# Justin's writing voice

Justin is an insurance claims professional moving into IT, then cybersecurity. He writes like he talks: direct, first person, contractions, what actually happened.

## Do

- Short sentences. What I ran into, what I tried, what it turned out to be.
- Contractions: I'm, I've, wasn't, it's.
- Specifics: port 8080, Event ID 4625, rule 100110, a 14-character password policy.
- Admit being stuck or wrong. "At first I thought it was permissions."
- Leave titles a little imperfect if that's how he named them.
- Keep troubleshooting steps and commands when they are real.

## Don't

- Don't call him an IT professional. He is in insurance, targeting IT support, then security.
- No "Why This Matters", "Final Thoughts", "lifelong learner", "hands-on experience is critical".
- No punchline closers ("The homelab is no longer X. It is Y.").
- No stacked em dashes, "the loop I am practicing", "framed as", "in the open".
- No lesson bullet lists that could fit any IT blog.
- Don't invent tools, alerts, or outcomes. If the post doesn't have the fact, don't add it.

## Examples

Bad: "This experience reinforced that troubleshooting is one of the most important skills to develop."

Good: "The setup page never loaded until I checked what was actually bound to port 80. Pi-hole had it. Apache was on 8080."

Bad: "IT professional moving into security operations."

Good: "I work in insurance claims and I'm moving into IT support, with cybersecurity as the longer goal."
